[[:db/retract 17592186220225 :answer-field/choices 17592186220226] [:db/retract 17592186220225 :answer-field/choices 17592186220227] [:db/retract 17592186220225 :answer-field/choices 17592186220228] [:db/retract 17592186220225 :answer-field/choices 17592186220229] [:db/retract 17592186220225 :answer-field/choices 17592186220230] [:db/retract 17592186220237 :answer-field/choices 17592186220240] [:db/retract 17592186220237 :answer-field/choices 17592186220241] [:db/retract 17592186220237 :answer-field/choices 17592186220242] [:db/retract 17592186220237 :answer-field/choices 17592186220238] [:db/retract 17592186220237 :answer-field/choices 17592186220239] {:db/id 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-056683d5-9c10-553b-93b1-01f7db0cfa24" "question-954d3e87-b1ac-516e-bb73-13e2611b178e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221157 :question/worked-solution "We know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t) = \\cos(\\pi t).$ Thus, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a9ec2e0a-7b21-59e9-965b-b9f812cb11e6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221158 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221432 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a4855477-bdfa-5214-b5d7-5a97d7d6e1ba" "question-72ddd4c8-8e72-5f66-b964-d1ec00d699bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221163 :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-056683d5-9c10-553b-93b1-01f7db0cfa24" :question/id #uuid "056683d5-9c10-553b-93b1-01f7db0cfa24" :question/math-academy-id "q-330828" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{(3/4)t}.$ Thus, for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{(3/4)t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{3}{4})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c3a0a01-7462-57a7-ba8f-e0b8d49c956f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c3a0a01-7462-57a7-ba8f-e0b8d49c956f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3a0a01-7462-57a7-ba8f-e0b8d49c956f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c3d659d-d6ec-5e9b-87ec-2f606fd5411d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b0fcf8bd-187b-515e-98be-9d23bc084284" "answer-20e282f4-88ec-52d6-8ea7-9a6576df4b1f" "answer-344220eb-e137-5ef4-9290-8367a1f2b18c" "answer-07d7257d-8558-5174-b584-bebeccdc3e51" "answer-3c3d659d-d6ec-5e9b-87ec-2f606fd5411d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0fcf8bd-187b-515e-98be-9d23bc084284" :answer/id #uuid "b0fcf8bd-187b-515e-98be-9d23bc084284" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4s-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20e282f4-88ec-52d6-8ea7-9a6576df4b1f" :answer/id #uuid "20e282f4-88ec-52d6-8ea7-9a6576df4b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4s-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-344220eb-e137-5ef4-9290-8367a1f2b18c" :answer/id #uuid "344220eb-e137-5ef4-9290-8367a1f2b18c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07d7257d-8558-5174-b584-bebeccdc3e51" :answer/id #uuid "07d7257d-8558-5174-b584-bebeccdc3e51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{4s-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c3d659d-d6ec-5e9b-87ec-2f606fd5411d" :answer/id #uuid "3c3d659d-d6ec-5e9b-87ec-2f606fd5411d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-954d3e87-b1ac-516e-bb73-13e2611b178e" :question/id #uuid "954d3e87-b1ac-516e-bb73-13e2611b178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-330821" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{3t}.$ Thus, for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61080eee-c12c-5456-b01c-6b7bdcb3d31a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61080eee-c12c-5456-b01c-6b7bdcb3d31a" :answer-field/id #uuid "61080eee-c12c-5456-b01c-6b7bdcb3d31a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8616a0a9-5e4c-5a5b-b120-d0f5a87babe9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8616a0a9-5e4c-5a5b-b120-d0f5a87babe9" "answer-ca93bd69-e78e-5e89-9ce8-c601d0049523" "answer-65f194c1-4a31-5dc6-9dd1-b74fb4074cce" "answer-e50db842-81cd-53f3-991f-23b6b0d1af22" "answer-f7e331c7-00aa-5a0a-a9ad-b24650546570"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8616a0a9-5e4c-5a5b-b120-d0f5a87babe9" :answer/id #uuid "8616a0a9-5e4c-5a5b-b120-d0f5a87babe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca93bd69-e78e-5e89-9ce8-c601d0049523" :answer/id #uuid "ca93bd69-e78e-5e89-9ce8-c601d0049523" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65f194c1-4a31-5dc6-9dd1-b74fb4074cce" :answer/id #uuid "65f194c1-4a31-5dc6-9dd1-b74fb4074cce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e50db842-81cd-53f3-991f-23b6b0d1af22" :answer/id #uuid "e50db842-81cd-53f3-991f-23b6b0d1af22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7e331c7-00aa-5a0a-a9ad-b24650546570" :answer/id #uuid "f7e331c7-00aa-5a0a-a9ad-b24650546570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304855 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221938 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{2}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220225 :answer-field/correct "answer-4b585c91-a64d-5156-9b47-3ab3be96c10e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a12e3278-78eb-5071-8f80-02016ffa5453" "answer-7393820c-ee1c-5fad-afb6-cfabaf3f9ba5" "answer-4b585c91-a64d-5156-9b47-3ab3be96c10e" "answer-31bff94b-20f3-5f6b-a517-e5ef54908756" "answer-d12a242e-7964-5cb0-85a3-8ccfe0400402"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a12e3278-78eb-5071-8f80-02016ffa5453" :answer/id #uuid "a12e3278-78eb-5071-8f80-02016ffa5453" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7393820c-ee1c-5fad-afb6-cfabaf3f9ba5" :answer/id #uuid "7393820c-ee1c-5fad-afb6-cfabaf3f9ba5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b585c91-a64d-5156-9b47-3ab3be96c10e" :answer/id #uuid "4b585c91-a64d-5156-9b47-3ab3be96c10e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31bff94b-20f3-5f6b-a517-e5ef54908756" :answer/id #uuid "31bff94b-20f3-5f6b-a517-e5ef54908756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d12a242e-7964-5cb0-85a3-8ccfe0400402" :answer/id #uuid "d12a242e-7964-5cb0-85a3-8ccfe0400402" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304853 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{3}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{3}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9ec2e0a-7b21-59e9-965b-b9f812cb11e6" :question/id #uuid "a9ec2e0a-7b21-59e9-965b-b9f812cb11e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-330846" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-4)},\\,s>4\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{4τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{4t}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-998aa03e-483d-5aca-ad28-ce026cba4873"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-998aa03e-483d-5aca-ad28-ce026cba4873" :answer-field/id #uuid "998aa03e-483d-5aca-ad28-ce026cba4873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-27a1e96a-2d02-5798-a435-19834cb6e962" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a067135-e5b7-55d1-925b-a9eb022c03a2" "answer-27a1e96a-2d02-5798-a435-19834cb6e962" "answer-27216b69-d2aa-588b-b25c-49b94f88c580" "answer-bdf7be69-473e-55c0-88f8-9515389c2337" "answer-eaa42c98-56c6-5b90-8cdd-cf61b5f56f21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a067135-e5b7-55d1-925b-a9eb022c03a2" :answer/id #uuid "3a067135-e5b7-55d1-925b-a9eb022c03a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-{e}^{4t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27a1e96a-2d02-5798-a435-19834cb6e962" :answer/id #uuid "27a1e96a-2d02-5798-a435-19834cb6e962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{4t}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27216b69-d2aa-588b-b25c-49b94f88c580" :answer/id #uuid "27216b69-d2aa-588b-b25c-49b94f88c580" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{t}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdf7be69-473e-55c0-88f8-9515389c2337" :answer/id #uuid "bdf7be69-473e-55c0-88f8-9515389c2337" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eaa42c98-56c6-5b90-8cdd-cf61b5f56f21" :answer/id #uuid "eaa42c98-56c6-5b90-8cdd-cf61b5f56f21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221940 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220237 :answer-field/correct "answer-10a07a90-d683-57da-8fc8-5a970843054f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60ee170d-1656-50f2-9512-e65dc86bd1f1" "answer-373e9c97-d5d1-54a7-a3e3-56e016bbff64" "answer-10a07a90-d683-57da-8fc8-5a970843054f" "answer-b052a9f1-3e7a-53f2-a98d-0235aab679a8" "answer-ebdb9b67-f22e-5c9d-b541-183acbc514e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60ee170d-1656-50f2-9512-e65dc86bd1f1" :answer/id #uuid "60ee170d-1656-50f2-9512-e65dc86bd1f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-373e9c97-d5d1-54a7-a3e3-56e016bbff64" :answer/id #uuid "373e9c97-d5d1-54a7-a3e3-56e016bbff64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10a07a90-d683-57da-8fc8-5a970843054f" :answer/id #uuid "10a07a90-d683-57da-8fc8-5a970843054f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b052a9f1-3e7a-53f2-a98d-0235aab679a8" :answer/id #uuid "b052a9f1-3e7a-53f2-a98d-0235aab679a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{4}}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebdb9b67-f22e-5c9d-b541-183acbc514e6" :answer/id #uuid "ebdb9b67-f22e-5c9d-b541-183acbc514e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304858 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{6!}{{s}^{6+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{6}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186302374 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304856 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a4855477-bdfa-5214-b5d7-5a97d7d6e1ba" :question/id #uuid "a4855477-bdfa-5214-b5d7-5a97d7d6e1ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-331077" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}$ given $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nLet $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ={{field-1}}\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}=L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={A}^{′}(t)\\text{ }dt \\\\ dv={{field-3}} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=$ {{field-5}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d135224-fa94-510a-b5c2-da161575c9f6" "answer-field-b671978e-add6-5314-969b-c27ee2b03359" "answer-field-6a91cc8a-1893-57a4-ab18-b6701b21fd12" "answer-field-942d23fb-8e37-5c51-8d6c-aba73e001424" "answer-field-bf1dea22-bf85-52ce-b7c9-10f7f0673293" "answer-field-a106189a-5468-5a47-ba2a-f4e4c6c746d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d135224-fa94-510a-b5c2-da161575c9f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d135224-fa94-510a-b5c2-da161575c9f6" :answer-field/key "fi