er/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b42be66f-2f5e-5b5e-8bde-f1bb580ea2f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "b42be66f-2f5e-5b5e-8bde-f1bb580ea2f4" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-33c2a665-68e3-5397-aad1-394033317b09" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46995870-fd5c-52bf-8d60-a3a53e6b049c" "answer-33c2a665-68e3-5397-aad1-394033317b09" "answer-2e92e92d-075f-5e99-a320-d6d09182f195" "answer-397f965b-6bb7-559f-abdf-3ffb9c3f2410"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46995870-fd5c-52bf-8d60-a3a53e6b049c" :answer/id #uuid "46995870-fd5c-52bf-8d60-a3a53e6b049c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(s)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33c2a665-68e3-5397-aad1-394033317b09" :answer/id #uuid "33c2a665-68e3-5397-aad1-394033317b09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{F(s)}{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e92e92d-075f-5e99-a320-d6d09182f195" :answer/id #uuid "2e92e92d-075f-5e99-a320-d6d09182f195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s}{F(s)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-397f965b-6bb7-559f-abdf-3ffb9c3f2410" :answer/id #uuid "397f965b-6bb7-559f-abdf-3ffb9c3f2410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sF(s)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222478 :tutorial/content "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau,\n\\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=e^{t/2}$. Thus, for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{t/2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\,\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ/2}\\,dτ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s\\,(s-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s(2s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221464 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
